From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 04:22:02 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] venus: let go of a large command and reply once the host has
 consumed them

A ring submission holds the shmems of its command and its reply until the
ring retires it, and the ring retires submissions only when the next one is
made. A thread that makes one large call and then no more, such as the
thread that saves an application's pipeline cache, therefore left its ring
holding the reply shmem of that call, up to hundreds of MiB mapped in the
host-visible aperture, until the thread exited. The upload buffer of a
large command was held the same way: the ring keeps it for the next upload,
and it is as large as the command.

After a call that waits for its reply, retire the ring's submissions up to
it when its reply or its command is larger than a pool serves. Release the
upload buffer of such a command as soon as it is submitted; the submission
holds it until the host has consumed it.

The test builds a ring on a renderer that only counts shmems and plays the
host, and checks that no large shmem outlives its call.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
